def register(add):
    def A(title,author,year,era,genre,setting,hook,cast,frames,q,answer,src,note='',date=None):
        url=f'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/{src}' if isinstance(src,int) else src
        add(title,author,year,date or str(year),era,genre,setting,hook,cast,frames,q,answer,source=url,note=note)
    A("Ozymandias","Percy Bysshe Shelley",1818,"romantic","Sonnet","An ancient ruin in a desert","A ruler commands eternity; the desert answers.","The speaker=Receives the account|The traveller=Describes the ruin|Ozymandias=King represented by a broken statue|The sculptor=Preserved the ruler's expression", '''
A report from afar~The speaker meets a traveller who describes an ancient landscape. The poem reaches its king through another person's account, immediately placing imperial authority at a distance.~The speaker,The traveller~road~POWER arrives as a report
The body is broken~Two enormous legs remain while the face lies damaged in the sand. The fragments promise greatness but also make visible the failure of the monument to preserve wholeness.~The traveller,Ozymandias~stone~A MONUMENT cannot stay whole
The expression survives~The sculptor captured contempt and commanding pride. Art preserves evidence of the ruler's temperament even when the actual political power that produced it has vanished.~Ozymandias,The sculptor~mirror~ART remembers the arrogance
The pedestal speaks~An inscription announces the king's extraordinary status and challenges rivals to compare achievements. Its certainty contrasts with the damaged body that now carries the words.~Ozymandias~crown~THE BOAST survives its empire
The reader looks around~No mighty works answer the command. The absence around the statue turns the expected proof of supremacy into evidence that supremacy has an ending.~The traveller~stone~ABSENCE reverses the claim
The sands extend~The landscape reaches beyond the human fragment in every direction. Time and the desert are not presented as obedient servants of the authority once asserted there.~The traveller~mountain~TIME outlasts command
The irony completes the portrait~The poem preserves the boast while changing its meaning. Readers remember Ozymandias because the monument fails, making art an unpredictable custodian of a ruler's intended legacy.~The speaker,The sculptor~book~IMMORTALITY arrives as irony''',"Why is the inscription ironic?","It demands admiration for lasting power while standing beside ruins surrounded by emptiness.", "https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/46565/ozymandias","A progression through the sonnet's framed description, not an invented biography of the king.")
    A("Ode to the West Wind","Percy Bysshe Shelley",1820,"romantic","Ode","An autumn landscape and the speaker's imagination","The force that scatters dead leaves might also scatter new ideas.","The speaker=Seeks renewed creative strength|The West Wind=Personified destroyer and preserver", '''
Leaves driven before the wind~Autumn's wind sweeps dead leaves away. The opening describes destruction vividly, but the same motion prepares for something beyond seasonal decay.~The speaker,The West Wind~forest~DESTRUCTION begins a cycle
Seeds await another season~The wind carries seeds to places where they lie dormant. What resembles burial is also preservation, making renewal dependent upon a period of apparent death.~The West Wind~garden~BURIAL can protect life
The sky is transformed~Clouds spread across the heavens like another mass of leaves. Storm imagery extends the wind's power from land to sky, giving the force a nearly cosmic scale.~The West Wind~storm~ONE FORCE crosses elements
The sea responds~The poem imagines the Mediterranean awakened and Atlantic depths shaken. Even regions beyond ordinary human sight become responsive to the wind's disturbing energy.~The West Wind~sea~POWER reaches below the surface
The speaker feels constrained~He wishes to share the wind's freedom, recalling earlier energy and recognising present suffering. The ode turns from praise of nature toward a personal appeal for release.~The speaker,The West Wind~chains~THE POET needs renewal
Make the poet an instrument~The speaker asks the wind to play through him and spread his thoughts. Poetry becomes something that might act in the world as forcefully as weather acts upon leaves and clouds.~The speaker,The West Wind~book~WORDS can travel outward
The question opens a future~The final seasonal question connects winter with the possibility of spring. Hope remains grounded in a cycle of destruction and renewal, rather than in the claim that pain has already disappeared.~The speaker~garden~CHANGE can carry hope''',"Why is the wind both destroyer and preserver?","It clears dead growth while carrying seeds that can germinate later.", "https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/45134/ode-to-the-west-wind","Written 1819; published 1820. A five-section ode; seven frames unpack its imagery and turn toward the speaker.",date="1820; written 1819")
    A("Prometheus Unbound","Percy Bysshe Shelley",1820,"romantic","Lyrical drama","A mythic landscape of oppression and release","A captive resists tyranny without becoming another tyrant.","Prometheus=Bound challenger of Jupiter|Jupiter=Divine tyrant|Asia=Prometheus's beloved|Panthea=Asia's companion|Demogorgon=Power connected with Jupiter's downfall", '''
The suffering captive~Prometheus endures punishment for opposing Jupiter. His physical bondage represents a wider order in which domination tries to make resistance collapse into submission or bitterness.~Prometheus,Jupiter~chains~TYRANNY punishes resistance
The curse is recalled~Prometheus hears his earlier curse against Jupiter but now withdraws its spirit of hatred. Refusing to reproduce the oppressor's cruelty becomes essential to the kind of freedom he seeks.~Prometheus~mirror~RESISTANCE need not imitate cruelty
Temptation and endurance~Agents of Jupiter offer pressure and visions of suffering. Prometheus refuses to yield, while sympathetic voices preserve the possibility that endurance has meaning beyond immediate pain.~Prometheus,Jupiter~storm~HOLD without surrendering compassion
Asia searches~Asia and Panthea move through an increasingly visionary landscape toward Demogorgon. Their journey links emotional and imaginative transformation to the question of how oppressive power can end.~Asia,Panthea~road~LOVE seeks understanding
Jupiter loses his throne~Jupiter imagines his rule secure, but Demogorgon confronts him and takes him down. The overthrow disrupts the assumption that established domination possesses an eternal right to continue.~Jupiter,Demogorgon~crown~APPARENT eternity collapses
Release and reunion~Prometheus is freed and reunited with Asia. The transformation extends through nature and human relations, presenting liberation as more than the replacement of one ruler by another.~Prometheus,Asia~garden~FREEDOM changes relationships
The imaginative world remade~The final act celebrates renewal while retaining the need for love, hope and endurance. Its ideal is moral and visionary transformation, not a practical account of a completed political revolution.~Prometheus,Asia,Demogorgon~stars~POWER is remade through love''',"Why does Prometheus withdraw his curse?","He refuses to make hatred the foundation of the freedom he wants.",4797,"A four-act lyrical drama. The transformed world is a poetic vision, not a historical event.")
    A("Ode to a Nightingale","John Keats",1819,"romantic","Ode","An imagined encounter with birdsong","A beautiful song offers escape, then returns us to uncertainty.","The speaker=Drawn toward and away from mortality|The nightingale=Singer imagined beyond human sorrow", '''
Joy feels like an ache~The speaker's intense response to the bird resembles numbness and pain. Its apparently effortless happiness exposes the emotional weight of his own human condition.~The speaker,The nightingale~forest~BEAUTY can hurt
An imagined cup of escape~He wishes for wine associated with warmth, festivity and distant landscapes. The desire is less for an ordinary drink than for a means of leaving weariness behind.~The speaker~coins~LONGING seeks an exit
The human world remembered~Illness, ageing and grief make human experience unstable. Remembering these conditions deepens the contrast between the speaker's vulnerability and the song he wants to follow.~The speaker~grave~MORTALITY shadows delight
Poetry replaces the wine~He turns toward imagination as a means of joining the bird. The night landscape becomes vivid through sounds and scents, with vision itself partly withheld.~The speaker,The nightingale~book~IMAGINATION changes the journey
Death becomes tempting~Listening in darkness, the speaker imagines dying at a moment of beauty. Yet the bird would go on singing while he would no longer be able to hear it.~The speaker,The nightingale~grave~ESCAPE would also end listening
The song crosses history~The nightingale's music seems to reach earlier listeners and imagined worlds. Its apparent immortality belongs to recurrent song rather than to the literal endless life of this individual bird.~The nightingale~stars~SONG outlasts a single listener
The music fades~The bird's departure breaks the imaginative experience. The speaker cannot clearly distinguish vision from dream, ending with a question rather than a triumphant conquest of mortality.~The speaker,The nightingale~forest~THE QUESTION remains awake''',"In what sense is the bird imagined as immortal?","Its recurring song seems to link listeners across time; the individual bird is not literally deathless.",23684,"First published 1819; included in the 1820 volume. The frames follow a lyric movement, not a physical expedition.")
    A("To Autumn","John Keats",1820,"romantic","Ode","Fields, orchards and an autumn evening","A season's passing becomes a reason to attend closely.","Autumn=Personified season|The observer=Attentive poetic viewpoint", '''
Ripeness fills the scene~Fruit, vines and crops reach fullness under the cooperation of season and sun. The opening dwells on abundance without needing to turn it into a story of personal possession.~Autumn,The observer~garden~FULLNESS asks for attention
The bees think warmth will last~Flowers continue to provide honey, and the bees seem to imagine an unending season. Their activity gently introduces the distance between present abundance and inevitable change.~Autumn~garden~DELIGHT does not stop time
The season rests~Autumn appears as a relaxed worker amid harvest. Personification makes the season tangible while slow gestures replace the urgent motion often associated with human ambition.~Autumn~home~A SEASON takes bodily form
The harvest has its pauses~Reaping, gleaning and the cider press show labour nearing completion. The poem values patient intervals and unfinished movements rather than only the final collected product.~Autumn,The observer~clock~PROCESS has its own beauty
Spring's song is not required~The poem asks about spring only to release the need for it. Autumn deserves attention on its own terms instead of being measured solely against a younger season.~The observer~book~VALUE the present season
Evening gathers its music~Gnats, lambs, crickets, birds and swallows form a layered soundscape. The season is not silent simply because it approaches an ending.~Autumn,The observer~forest~PASSING life still sings
The ending holds movement~Gathering swallows suggest departure without canceling the scene's beauty. Acceptance emerges through sustained observation, allowing fullness and decline to coexist.~The observer~stars~ABUNDANCE and ending together''',"Why does the poem not need the songs of spring?","Autumn has its own beauty and music, worthy of attention without comparison to renewal.",23684,"Written September 1819; published 1820. Personified figures are memory aids for a non-narrative poem.",date="1820; written 1819")
    A("Don Juan","Lord Byron",1819,"romantic","Satirical verse narrative","Spain, the Mediterranean, Russia and England","An adventurous young man exposes the world's confident pretences.","Juan=Young traveller often acted upon by others|Donna Julia=Early lover|Haidée=Rescues and loves Juan|Lambro=Haidée's father|The narrator=Digressive and satirical guide", '''
The hero is proposed~The narrator chooses Juan and immediately unsettles the conventional heroic opening. His comments on society, poetry and morality compete with the adventures he supposedly wants to narrate.~Juan,The narrator~book~THE TELLING is part of the story
An education meets desire~Juan's upbringing does not protect him from an affair with Donna Julia. Discovery sends him away from Spain, making scandal rather than noble purpose the start of his travel.~Juan,Donna Julia~heart~RESPECTABILITY cannot contain desire
Shipwreck strips away civilisation~The sea destroys the familiar social world. Hunger and extreme suffering expose how fragile ordinary moral confidence can become when survival replaces comfort.~Juan~sea~NECESSITY tests appearances
Haidée offers a different life~Rescued by Haidée, Juan enters an island romance that appears removed from conventional hypocrisy. Their happiness nevertheless depends on an unstable temporary absence of paternal authority.~Juan,Haidée~garden~AN IDYLL has a condition
Lambro returns~Haidée's father breaks the lovers' world and Juan is sold onward. Haidée's tragic fate contrasts with the mobile hero's continued adventures, showing that consequences are not distributed equally.~Juan,Haidée,Lambro~chains~ROMANCE meets ownership
Empires and fashionable life~Juan moves through the Ottoman court, war, Catherine's Russia and English high society. Institutions that claim grandeur appear entangled with appetite, vanity and opportunism.~Juan,The narrator~crown~POWER wears respectable costumes
The narrator keeps digressing~The unfinished poem does not provide a final moral settlement. Its humour repeatedly interrupts certainty, asking readers to distrust polished accounts of heroism and social virtue.~The narrator,Juan~road~SATIRE refuses a tidy destination''',"How does Byron's Juan differ from a purely active seducer?","He is often acted upon and carried through circumstances, while the narrator uses his travels to satirise society.",21700,"Published in stages from 1819 to 1824 and left unfinished.",date="1819–1824")
    A("Goblin Market","Christina Rossetti",1862,"victorian","Narrative poem","A household beside a mysterious fruit market","Temptation isolates one sister; the other risks herself to bring her back.","Laura=Eats the goblins' fruit|Lizzie=Protective sister|The goblins=Enticing fruit merchants|Jeanie=Remembered earlier victim", '''
The merchants call~Goblins advertise an abundance of tempting fruit. Lizzie warns Laura against answering them, making sisterly care the first counterweight to the attraction of the market.~Laura,Lizzie,The goblins~garden~DESIRE hears a promise
A purchase without money~Laura exchanges a lock of hair for fruit and eats intensely. The transaction binds bodily desire to a marketplace whose terms she does not understand.~Laura,The goblins~coins~THE PRICE is personal
The song disappears~Afterward Laura longs for more but can no longer hear the sellers. Lizzie still hears them, and the unequal access turns shared daily life into Laura's lonely fixation.~Laura,Lizzie~forest~CRAVING creates isolation
The warning takes shape~Laura weakens, recalling the fate of Jeanie, another girl who ate the fruit. Memory now supplies evidence of danger, but knowledge alone cannot undo the craving.~Laura,Jeanie~grave~A PREVIOUS life gives warning
Lizzie enters the market~Lizzie takes a coin and goes to obtain help. The goblins insist she eat, then attack when she refuses, exposing coercion beneath the earlier language of invitation.~Lizzie,The goblins~chains~A MARKET can turn violent
The rescue carries the taste~Lizzie returns with juice on her body and invites Laura to take it. Laura experiences a painful crisis before recovery, turning the substance associated with harm into part of her rescue.~Laura,Lizzie~heart~CARE bears the risk
The sisters remember~Later, as mothers, they retell their experience and affirm sisterly solidarity. The ending makes a shared story of protection out of an ordeal that first produced isolation.~Laura,Lizzie~book~REMEMBER who came back for you''',"What distinguishes Lizzie's visit from Laura's?","Lizzie refuses the merchants' terms and acts to help her sister rather than satisfy her own craving.",16950,"The poem supports multiple religious, economic and gender interpretations; no single allegory exhausts it.")
    A("Pride and Prejudice","Jane Austen",1813,"romantic","Novel of manners","Hertfordshire, Kent and Derbyshire","First impressions must survive the evidence of character.","Elizabeth Bennet=Independent judge of people|Mr. Darcy=Proud wealthy suitor|Jane Bennet=Elizabeth's gentle sister|Mr. Bingley=Loves Jane|Wickham=Charming and deceptive|Lydia Bennet=Impulsive younger sister", '''
A new neighbour changes expectations~Bingley's arrival encourages the Bennet family's marriage hopes. Darcy's reserved pride offends Elizabeth, and she begins to read his character through the injury of their first encounter.~Elizabeth Bennet,Mr. Darcy,Mr. Bingley~hall~AN IMPRESSION hardens quickly
Charm supplies a rival account~Wickham offers an attractive story about Darcy's supposed wrongdoing. Elizabeth believes it readily, while Jane and Bingley's affection is interrupted by social interference.~Elizabeth Bennet,Wickham,Jane Bennet~mask~LIKING can distort judgement
Marriage has material stakes~Elizabeth rejects Mr. Collins, but Charlotte accepts him for security. The contrast makes choice look different for women with different resources and expectations.~Elizabeth Bennet~coins~FREEDOM depends on circumstances
The first proposal fails~Darcy proposes while emphasising the objections he has overcome. Elizabeth condemns his treatment of Jane and Wickham, rejecting an offer whose manner seems to confirm his arrogance.~Elizabeth Bennet,Mr. Darcy~heart~PRIDE ruins the appeal
The letter changes the evidence~Darcy explains Wickham's conduct and his role in Bingley's withdrawal. Elizabeth begins to recognise how prejudice and vanity have shaped the confidence of her own judgements.~Elizabeth Bennet,Mr. Darcy,Wickham~letter~REVISION needs evidence
Lydia's crisis tests action~Lydia runs away with Wickham, threatening family reputation. Darcy quietly helps secure the marriage, showing through costly action a generosity that earlier appearances concealed.~Mr. Darcy,Lydia Bennet,Wickham~contract~CHARACTER is seen in deeds
Both learn to reconsider~Bingley and Jane reunite, and Elizabeth accepts a changed Darcy. Their marriage follows mutual self-correction, joining affection to better understanding rather than merely to wealth.~Elizabeth Bennet,Mr. Darcy,Jane Bennet~garden~LOVE grows through revision''',"What makes Elizabeth revise her view of Darcy?","His explanatory letter and later actions reveal the weaknesses of her first impressions.",1342)
    A("Sense and Sensibility","Jane Austen",1811,"romantic","Novel of manners","Sussex, Devon and London","Two sisters learn that feeling and judgement need each other.","Elinor Dashwood=Restrained elder sister|Marianne Dashwood=Openly passionate sister|Edward Ferrars=Elinor's attachment|Willoughby=Marianne's charming suitor|Colonel Brandon=Loyal admirer|Lucy Steele=Holds Edward's secret engagement", '''
The home is lost~Inheritance leaves the Dashwood women with reduced resources. Moving to a smaller home changes the sisters' prospects, making emotion inseparable from the material arrangements governing family life.~Elinor Dashwood,Marianne Dashwood~home~INHERITANCE shapes the choices
Two kinds of attachment~Elinor feels for Edward but reads uncertain signs cautiously. Marianne meets Willoughby and treats their shared tastes and intense attraction as evidence of a perfect understanding.~Elinor Dashwood,Marianne Dashwood,Willoughby~heart~FEELING takes different forms
The hidden engagement~Lucy reveals that she is already engaged to Edward. Elinor must keep the confidence and bear disappointment privately, so her apparent composure conceals considerable emotional labour.~Elinor Dashwood,Lucy Steele,Edward Ferrars~contract~RESTRAINT is not absence of pain
A public humiliation~In London, Willoughby rejects Marianne and marries for wealth. His earlier warmth proves compatible with selfish choices, breaking her belief that intense feeling guarantees trustworthy conduct.~Marianne Dashwood,Willoughby~letter~CHARM is not commitment
Brandon's history reframes the man~Colonel Brandon reveals Willoughby's treatment of another young woman. The disclosure makes the betrayal more than a romance gone wrong and exposes a pattern of irresponsibility.~Colonel Brandon,Marianne Dashwood~book~A HISTORY changes the portrait
Illness and self-recognition~Marianne's illness brings the sisters close to loss. Recovery leads her to reconsider her earlier conduct without requiring her to abandon the capacity for strong feeling.~Marianne Dashwood,Elinor Dashwood~bed~REFLECTION can follow suffering
The unexpected release~Lucy marries Edward's brother, freeing Edward to propose to Elinor. Marianne eventually marries Brandon; the endings suggest the value of combining affection with responsible attention.~Elinor Dashwood,Edward Ferrars,Marianne Dashwood~garden~FEELING and judgement can cooperate''',"Why should Elinor's restraint not be mistaken for coldness?","She experiences deep pain but bears it privately and considers the needs of others.",161)
    A("Emma","Jane Austen",1815,"romantic","Novel of manners","Highbury in England","A confident matchmaker must learn how little she knows.","Emma Woodhouse=Privileged and mistaken matchmaker|Harriet Smith=Friend influenced by Emma|Mr. Knightley=Critical friend and eventual partner|Frank Churchill=Conceals an engagement|Jane Fairfax=Woman with limited prospects|Miss Bates=Vulnerable neighbour", '''
The matchmaker feels certain~Emma credits herself with arranging a successful marriage and turns toward Harriet's future. Wealth and security let her treat other people's prospects as an entertaining field for intervention.~Emma Woodhouse,Harriet Smith~heart~PRIVILEGE encourages confidence
The farmer is discouraged~Emma persuades Harriet to reject Robert Martin despite his genuine affection. Her assumptions about rank outweigh the evidence of a suitable and caring relationship.~Emma Woodhouse,Harriet Smith,Mr. Knightley~coins~STATUS overrides good evidence
Mr. Elton reveals the mistake~Emma imagines Elton loves Harriet, but he proposes to Emma instead. The surprise exposes how easily she interprets another person's conduct through the plot she wants to construct.~Emma Woodhouse,Harriet Smith~mirror~THE STORY was hers not his
Frank supplies more misdirection~Frank Churchill flirts while secretly engaged to Jane Fairfax. Emma enjoys the attention and misunderstands Jane, whose position requires much greater caution and discretion.~Emma Woodhouse,Frank Churchill,Jane Fairfax~mask~PLAYFUL appearances hide serious stakes
Box Hill wounds Miss Bates~Emma makes a cutting joke at Miss Bates's expense. Knightley's rebuke forces her to see the cruelty of exercising social ease against someone with little protection.~Emma Woodhouse,Miss Bates,Mr. Knightley~storm~WIT can injure the vulnerable
Harriet changes Emma's view~When Harriet speaks of Knightley, Emma recognises her own feelings and fears the consequences of further interference. The emotional shock makes her imagined mastery of relationships collapse.~Emma Woodhouse,Harriet Smith,Mr. Knightley~heart~SELF-KNOWLEDGE arrives through jealousy
Repair through attention~Knightley loves Emma, Harriet returns to Martin, and the concealed engagement is explained. Emma's growth depends on humility and practical care rather than the triumph of her matchmaking.~Emma Woodhouse,Mr. Knightley,Harriet Smith~garden~LESS certainty more care''',"What does the Box Hill episode teach Emma?","That cleverness and privilege can become cruelty when directed at a vulnerable person.",158,"Published December 1815 with a title page dated 1816.",date="1815; title page 1816")
    A("Jane Eyre","Charlotte Brontë",1847,"victorian","Gothic novel","Gateshead, Lowood, Thornfield and Ferndean","A woman seeks love without surrendering self-respect.","Jane Eyre=Orphan and independent narrator|Rochester=Thornfield's master|Helen Burns=Jane's school friend|Bertha Mason=Rochester's confined wife|St. John Rivers=Missionary and cousin", '''
The child resists humiliation~At Gateshead, Jane experiences exclusion and punishment, including the terrifying red room. Her anger asserts a self that the household would prefer to keep silent and dependent.~Jane Eyre~home~THE VOICE refuses erasure
Lowood teaches and wounds~The harsh school exposes hypocrisy and neglect. Helen Burns's friendship matters deeply, but her death also teaches Jane that affection cannot always protect the vulnerable.~Jane Eyre,Helen Burns~grave~LEARNING carries loss
A governess at Thornfield~Jane becomes a teacher in Rochester's household. Their conversations create intimacy across rank, while strange disturbances remind her that the house contains a history she does not know.~Jane Eyre,Rochester~castle~CONNECTION beneath a mystery
The marriage is interrupted~At the wedding, Jane learns that Rochester already has a living wife, Bertha, confined at Thornfield. His account cannot make the proposed marriage compatible with Jane's conscience.~Jane Eyre,Rochester,Bertha Mason~contract~LOVE cannot erase another life
Jane chooses departure~She leaves despite intense attachment and faces poverty before finding help. Discovering family ties and an inheritance later gives her a different basis for independence.~Jane Eyre,St. John Rivers~road~SELF-RESPECT accepts a cost
Another unequal offer~St. John wants Jane as a missionary wife without romantic love. She refuses a role that would consume her identity under the language of duty, resisting a different form of domination.~Jane Eyre,St. John Rivers~chains~DUTY also needs consent
Return on changed terms~Jane returns after Thornfield's fire and Bertha's death. Rochester is injured; Jane now has independent resources, and their marriage follows a changed balance of choice and dependence.~Jane Eyre,Rochester~garden~LOVE with the self intact''',"Why does Jane leave Rochester after the interrupted wedding?","She refuses to sacrifice her conscience and self-respect even for a person she loves.",1260,"Bertha's representation and confinement raise important questions about race, disability and whose story the narrator can tell.")
    A("Wuthering Heights","Emily Brontë",1847,"victorian","Gothic novel","Two houses on the Yorkshire moors","Love becomes possession; revenge reaches a second generation.","Heathcliff=Outsider turned avenger|Catherine Earnshaw=His intense childhood companion|Edgar Linton=Catherine's husband|Cathy Linton=Their daughter|Hareton Earnshaw=Young man denied education|Nelly Dean=Major narrator", '''
The stranger hears a history~Lockwood encounters the harsh household of Wuthering Heights and asks Nelly about its past. The story reaches readers through layered narrators whose judgements remain open to question.~Nelly Dean,Heathcliff~home~THE PAST comes through witnesses
An outsider in the family~Heathcliff is brought into the Earnshaw household. Catherine shares his wild childhood freedom, while Hindley's hostility subjects him to humiliation and exclusion.~Heathcliff,Catherine Earnshaw~forest~AFFECTION beside degradation
Catherine chooses social safety~She marries Edgar while claiming a deeper identification with Heathcliff. The choice divides social advancement from emotional allegiance and leaves both attachments painfully unresolved.~Catherine Earnshaw,Edgar Linton,Heathcliff~heart~TWO futures cannot be reconciled
The return intensifies conflict~Heathcliff comes back with resources and pursues revenge against the households that hurt him. Catherine's illness and death do not extinguish his attachment or his desire to punish others.~Heathcliff,Catherine Earnshaw~storm~LOSS feeds possession
The next generation is trapped~Heathcliff gains property and manipulates young people through debt, marriage and withheld education. Cathy and Hareton inherit the injuries of conflicts they did not begin.~Heathcliff,Cathy Linton,Hareton Earnshaw~chains~REVENGE outlives its first targets
Learning offers another relation~Cathy and Hareton move from contempt toward affection and shared reading. Their bond presents an alternative to the coercive forms of attachment that governed the earlier generation.~Cathy Linton,Hareton Earnshaw~book~CARE can interrupt inheritance
The avenger loses his aim~Heathcliff's obsession with Catherine overwhelms his interest in further revenge, and he dies. The younger couple looks toward a future while rumours keep the earlier lovers haunting the landscape.~Heathcliff,Cathy Linton,Hareton Earnshaw~grave~THE CYCLE may finally break''',"What makes Cathy and Hareton's relationship different?","Mutual learning and affection offer a way out of the earlier pattern of humiliation and coercion.",768,"The nested narration complicates certainty; the supernatural ending remains suggestive rather than conclusively explained.")
    A("Great Expectations","Charles Dickens",1860,"victorian","Coming-of-age novel","Kent and London","A fortune exposes what a boy has mistaken for worth.","Pip=Ambitious orphan narrator|Joe Gargery=Loyal blacksmith|Estella=Woman Pip idealises|Miss Havisham=Embittered patron|Magwitch=Convict and benefactor|Herbert Pocket=Friend in London", '''
The convict on the marsh~Young Pip brings food and a file to a desperate escaped prisoner. Fear drives his assistance, but that small act will later become decisive in a way he cannot imagine.~Pip,Magwitch~chains~ONE ACT returns years later
Satis House creates shame~Pip meets Miss Havisham and Estella. Estella's contempt makes his ordinary home and Joe's kindness seem inadequate, tying social ambition to an injured sense of self.~Pip,Estella,Miss Havisham~mirror~DESIRE teaches him to despise home
The expectations arrive~An unnamed benefactor finances Pip's life as a gentleman. He assumes Miss Havisham intends him for Estella, constructing a hopeful explanation from evidence that never actually proves it.~Pip,Miss Havisham~coins~A FORTUNE invites a fantasy
London changes his conduct~Pip learns fashionable habits and accumulates debt with Herbert. His embarrassment about Joe exposes how easily advancement can weaken gratitude toward the people who have cared for him.~Pip,Herbert Pocket,Joe Gargery~city~STATUS can obscure loyalty
The benefactor is revealed~Magwitch returns and identifies himself as the source of Pip's money. The discovery destroys Pip's imagined romance and makes his comfort depend on someone society marks as criminal.~Pip,Magwitch~letter~THE SOURCE overturns the dream
Care replaces disgust~Pip tries to help Magwitch escape and comes to recognise his humanity. Arrest and death defeat the practical plan, but Pip's response demonstrates growth beyond his earlier class prejudice.~Pip,Magwitch~ship~GRATITUDE becomes responsibility
The expectations are reconsidered~Joe nurses Pip and helps with his debts. Pip builds a humbler life; the revised ending brings a meeting with Estella without guaranteeing a simple restoration of his youthful fantasy.~Pip,Joe Gargery,Estella~garden~WORTH is not a social costume''',"Who is Pip's actual benefactor?","Magwitch, the convict Pip helped as a child, rather than Miss Havisham.",1400,"Serialised 1860–1861. Dickens wrote two endings; the widely printed revised ending remains open to interpretation.",date="1860–1861")
    A("A Tale of Two Cities","Charles Dickens",1859,"victorian","Historical novel","London and Paris around the French Revolution","Private sacrifice confronts public cycles of revenge.","Dr. Manette=Former prisoner restored to family|Lucie Manette=His devoted daughter|Charles Darnay=Rejects his aristocratic inheritance|Sydney Carton=Disillusioned lawyer|Madame Defarge=Organiser of revolutionary vengeance", '''
A prisoner is recalled to life~Dr. Manette is recovered after long confinement in the Bastille. Lucie's care begins his restoration, joining a political injustice to the intimate labour of helping a damaged person live again.~Dr. Manette,Lucie Manette~chains~CARE makes return possible
Two men look alike~Darnay's trial in London reveals his resemblance to Carton. The similarity saves him and establishes a connection whose importance exceeds the immediate legal episode.~Charles Darnay,Sydney Carton~mirror~A RESEMBLANCE plants a possibility
A rejected inheritance~Darnay distances himself from his French aristocratic family and marries Lucie. Personal decency, however, cannot automatically release him from the historical injuries attached to his name.~Charles Darnay,Lucie Manette~contract~A NAME carries history
Revolution answers cruelty~The old order's violence provokes revolt, and Madame Defarge records enemies. Liberation from oppression begins to turn into a system of inherited vengeance.~Madame Defarge~storm~JUSTICE can become revenge
The past becomes an accusation~Returning to aid a servant, Darnay is imprisoned. Manette's old written testimony exposes the crimes of Darnay's family and helps condemn the man Lucie loves.~Charles Darnay,Dr. Manette~letter~THE RECORD returns against the family
Carton changes the outcome~Carton uses the resemblance to substitute himself for Darnay. His earlier promise of devotion to Lucie becomes an action that offers another man and family a future.~Sydney Carton,Charles Darnay,Lucie Manette~gate~LOVE acts without possession
The sacrifice looks forward~Carton dies at the guillotine with a vision of renewal beyond the present violence. The ending opposes his voluntary sacrifice to the compulsive revenge consuming revolutionary society.~Sydney Carton~grave~ONE LIFE opens a future''',"What lets Carton save Darnay?","Their physical resemblance allows Carton to take his place in prison and at execution.",98)
    A("Oliver Twist","Charles Dickens",1837,"victorian","Social novel","Workhouses and London","An orphan's vulnerability reveals the cruelty behind respectable systems.","Oliver=Orphan seeking care|Fagin=Organises child theft|Nancy=Protects Oliver despite danger|Bill Sikes=Violent criminal|Mr. Brownlow=Offers safety|Monks=Plots against Oliver", '''
The workhouse child~Oliver grows up under punitive charity that treats need as a moral fault. His request for more food becomes scandalous because the institution expects gratitude instead of a voice.~Oliver~home~NEED is treated as misconduct
Escape into another system~After misery in an apprenticeship, Oliver runs to London and meets the Artful Dodger. Fagin's household appears welcoming before its business of training children to steal becomes clear.~Oliver,Fagin~city~SHELTER can conceal exploitation
Brownlow offers an alternative~Mistakenly caught after a theft, Oliver is helped by Brownlow. Kindness briefly interrupts the criminal world's claim over him, showing that protection changes what choices a child can make.~Oliver,Mr. Brownlow~garden~CARE opens another path
The gang takes him back~Nancy and Sikes help recapture Oliver, and he is forced into a burglary. Injury during the attempt draws him toward another protective household rather than confirming the criminal identity imposed on him.~Oliver,Nancy,Bill Sikes~chains~COERCION is not character
Nancy risks a disclosure~Nancy reveals a plot connecting Monks to Oliver's origins. Helping him requires crossing the loyalties and fears that keep her bound to Sikes's world.~Nancy,Monks~letter~KNOWLEDGE becomes an act of care
Violence silences the helper~Sikes murders Nancy after suspecting betrayal. His flight ends in death, while the machinery surrounding Fagin is exposed; Oliver's rescue carries the cost borne by someone who could not escape herself.~Nancy,Bill Sikes,Fagin~grave~RESCUE has a terrible price
Origins and protection~The truth of Oliver's parentage and Monks's scheme emerges. Brownlow adopts Oliver, but the novel's indictment reaches beyond one fortunate ending to the institutions that endangered children like him.~Oliver,Mr. Brownlow,Monks~home~ONE RESCUE cannot justify the system''',"Why is Nancy central to Oliver's rescue?","She supplies crucial information at personal risk, even though she remains trapped in the criminal world.",730,"Serialised 1837–1839. Fagin's antisemitic depiction is part of the novel's historical prejudice and should be examined critically.",date="1837–1839")
    A("Bleak House","Charles Dickens",1852,"victorian","Social and legal novel","London and the English countryside","An endless lawsuit consumes lives while a hidden history seeks recognition.","Esther Summerson=Modest first-person narrator|John Jarndyce=Protective guardian|Richard Carstone=Absorbed by the lawsuit|Ada Clare=Richard's loving partner|Lady Dedlock=Conceals Esther's parentage|Inspector Bucket=Investigates the mystery|Jo=Neglected street child", '''
Fog fills the court~The opening's fog surrounds Chancery and the interminable case of Jarndyce and Jarndyce. Legal complexity becomes an atmosphere affecting people who can scarcely understand or control it.~John Jarndyce,Richard Carstone~city~A SYSTEM obscures its own harm
Esther finds a household~Esther joins Jarndyce's home with Ada and Richard. Her care and self-effacing account create one perspective beside the novel's sharper third-person view of institutions.~Esther Summerson,Ada Clare,John Jarndyce~home~CARE works within uncertainty
Richard waits for a fortune~Richard increasingly invests his hopes in the lawsuit. What appears to promise independence instead drains his attention, resources and ability to build a life outside its expected reward.~Richard Carstone,Ada Clare~coins~EXPECTATION becomes captivity
The secret mother~Clues surrounding a dead law-writer connect Lady Dedlock with Esther. A concealed personal history becomes vulnerable to investigation and the coercive uses of legal knowledge.~Lady Dedlock,Esther Summerson~letter~THE PRIVATE life has a trace
The neglected child carries consequence~Jo's poverty and illness show how social abandonment moves across apparently separate worlds. Esther's infection makes suffering impossible to keep safely outside respectable households.~Jo,Esther Summerson~chains~NEGLECT does not stay elsewhere
The search ends in loss~Bucket follows Lady Dedlock after exposure drives her from safety. Esther reaches her too late, turning the pursuit of truth into an encounter with a protection that failed.~Inspector Bucket,Lady Dedlock,Esther Summerson~grave~DISCOVERY cannot restore the dead
The case consumes its prize~The estate is exhausted by costs, and Richard dies after losing his hope. Esther's later marriage offers private care, but the ending leaves the institutional waste unmistakable.~Richard Carstone,Esther Summerson,Ada Clare~book~THE PROCESS eats the purpose''',"What finally happens to the money in the lawsuit?","Legal costs consume the estate, destroying the promise on which Richard has built his hopes.",1023,"Serialised 1852–1853. Alternating narrators are central to how the novel connects personal care and systemic criticism.",date="1852–1853")
